/**
 * @defgroup StSvtSimulationMaker StSvtSimulationMaker — SVT Slow Simulator
 * @{
 *
 * @brief Full slow simulation of the STAR Silicon Vertex Tracker (SVT),
 *        a Silicon Drift Detector (SDD) system.
 *
 * @details
 * ## SVT Detector Overview
 *
 * The STAR SVT is a three-barrel silicon drift detector surrounding the
 * collision point.  Each barrel is made of silicon wafers divided into two
 * hybrids per wafer.  Each hybrid contains 240 anodes and 128 time bins,
 * giving a 240×128 pixel grid per hybrid.
 *
 * | Barrel | Layers | Ladders | Wafers | Hybrids |
 * |--------|--------|---------|--------|---------|
 * | 1      | 1–2    | 4       | 4      | 2       |
 * | 2      | 3–4    | 6       | 6      | 2       |
 * | 3      | 5–6    | 8       | 7      | 2       |
 *
 * In a Silicon Drift Detector the ionisation electrons do **not** drift
 * straight to the back surface; instead an internal lateral electric field
 * drives them sideways toward a row of collection anodes at one end of the
 * wafer.  The drift direction is therefore approximately along the beam axis
 * (X in local coordinates), and the anodes are oriented transverse to it.
 *
 * ## Simulation Pipeline
 *
 * The simulation follows these steps in order:
 *
 * ### Step 1 — GEANT Hit Ingestion
 * StSvtSimulationMaker::Make() reads Monte-Carlo hits from the
 * `StMcSvtHitCollection` produced by GEANT.  For every hit the global
 * position and local momentum are extracted.
 *
 * ### Step 2 — Ideal-to-Real Geometry Translation
 * Because GEANT uses an idealised geometry while the actual wafers have
 * survey-measured positions, StSvtSimulationMaker::ideal2RealTranslation()
 * projects the hit from the ideal surface onto the nearest real wafer using
 * the StPhysicalHelixD track helix and the actual magnetic field.
 *
 * ### Step 3 — Coordinate Transformation
 * The global hit position is converted to local (barrel/wafer) coordinates
 * via StSvtCoordinateTransform, then to detector (anode, time-bucket)
 * coordinates.  An iterative correction loop ensures consistency between the
 * local↔wafer round-trip given the per-hybrid drift-velocity mapping.
 *
 * ### Step 4 — Track Angle Calculation
 * StSvtAngles::calcAngles() computes the polar angle θ (between the track
 * momentum and the wafer normal **n**) and the azimuthal angle φ (in the
 * wafer plane, measured from the drift direction **d**).  These angles
 * determine the initial shape of the ionisation electron cloud.
 *
 * ### Step 5 — Ionisation and Electron-Cloud Expansion
 * StSvtElectronCloud::setPar() initialises the total charge:
 * @f[
 *   Q_0 = \frac{\Delta E}{\varepsilon_{\rm Si}} \times
 *         \Bigl(1 - \frac{2\,w_0\,|\sin\theta|}{3\,d_{\rm SDD}}\Bigr)
 * @f]
 * where @f$\varepsilon_{\rm Si}=3.6\;\text{eV}@f$ is the mean ionisation
 * energy in silicon and @f$d_{\rm SDD}=0.28\;\text{mm}@f$ is the wafer
 * thickness.  The correction factor removes the fraction of the track
 * path that exits the wafer before all charge is created.
 *
 * The three variance components of the cloud
 * @f$(\sigma_X^2,\sigma_Y^2,\sigma_{XY}^2)@f$ evolve under combined
 * thermal diffusion and space-charge (Coulomb) repulsion.  The equations
 * of motion for the *cube roots* @f$(S_X,S_Y,S_{XY})@f$ of the variances are:
 * @f{align*}{
 *   \dot S_X    &= \tfrac{3}{2}S_X   \bigl[2D_X      + \tfrac{1}{2}(f_1+f_2 + (f_1-f_2)\cos 2\phi)\bigr] \\
 *   \dot S_Y    &= \tfrac{3}{2}S_Y   \bigl[2D_Y      + \tfrac{1}{2}(f_1+f_2 - (f_1-f_2)\cos 2\phi)\bigr] \\
 *   \dot S_{XY} &= \tfrac{3}{2}S_{XY}\bigl[\cdots + \tfrac{1}{2}(f_1-f_2)\sin 2\phi\bigr]
 * @f}
 * where @f$D_X,D_Y@f$ are the diffusion constants (identical without
 * trapping, enhanced along drift when trapping is present) and @f$f_1,f_2@f$
 * are the two space-charge force functions that depend on the
 * minor-to-major axis ratio @f$r=\sigma_{\min}/\sigma_{\maj}@f$ and the
 * normalised minor radius @f$v_d = \sigma_{\min}/d_{\rm SDD}@f$.
 *
 * Integration is performed by a hybrid Runge–Kutta 4 / Adams–Bashforth–
 * Moulton 4-step predictor–corrector scheme:
 * - Short drift times use pure RK4.
 * - Long drift times use three RK4 bootstrap steps, then the ABM
 *   predictor–corrector at larger step sizes for efficiency.
 *
 * Charge attenuation during drift:
 * @f[
 *   Q(t) = Q_0\,e^{-t/\tau_{\rm life}}
 * @f]
 *
 * ### Step 6 — Anode Charge Fraction
 * StSvtSignal::chargeFraction() partitions the collected charge among the
 * anodes surrounding the hit using the complementary error function (Gaussian
 * integral) of the cloud's σ_Y (anode-direction width).
 *
 * ### Step 7 — PASA Signal Shaping
 * The charge deposited on each anode is convolved with the PASA (Pre-
 * Amplifier Shaper Amplifier) impulse response.  The PASA is modelled as a
 * bipolar shaper:
 * @f[
 *   h(t) = \Bigl(b_5 e^{-bt} + P(t)\,e^{-at}\Bigr)\,\Theta(t)
 * @f]
 * where @f$a=1/\tau_s=90.9\;\mu\text{s}^{-1}@f$ (short time constant,
 * @f$\tau_s=11\;\text{ns}@f$) and @f$b=1/\tau_l=2\;\mu\text{s}^{-1}@f$
 * (long time constant, @f$\tau_l=500\;\text{ns}@f$), and @f$P(t)@f$ is a
 * degree-4 polynomial in @f$t@f$.  The convolution with a Gaussian input of
 * width @f$\sigma_t@f$ is computed analytically for narrow clouds
 * (StSvtSignal::getShortSignal()) and via a closed-form integral of the
 * Gaussian×PASA product for wide clouds (StSvtSignal::getLongSignal()).
 * Gain is @f$G=13.4\;\mu\text{V/e}@f$.
 *
 * ### Step 8 — ADC Digitisation
 * The shaped signal in mV is scaled to counts:
 * @f[
 *   \text{counts} = V_{\rm mV} / 3.90625
 * @f]
 * (1 count = 4 mV × 1000/1024 in 10-bit mode).  The pedestal offset is
 * added, and the result is stored as a double-precision pixel array.
 *
 * ### Step 9 — Embedding and Background (StSvtEmbeddingMaker)
 * In true-embedding mode, real raw-data sequences from the DAQ reader are
 * added directly to the simulated pixel array.  Background Gaussian noise
 * is then added under the simulated hits (and everywhere in plain-simulation
 * mode) using per-pixel or per-hybrid RMS values from the database, or a
 * fallback default of σ = 1.8 counts.
 *
 * ### Step 10 — Online DAQ Simulation (StSvtOnlineSeqAdjSimMaker)
 * - The 10-bit pixel array is compressed to 8 bits via the lookup table
 *   in StSvtConversionTable.h (linear for codes 0–127, then 3-to-1 average
 *   for codes 128–895, then saturation at 255).
 * - Bad anodes (from the database) are zeroed.
 * - The first `N` time bins are cleared (DAQ blanking, default N=2).
 * - Sequence search: a two-threshold algorithm with minimum sequence lengths
 *   @f$n_{\rm lo}@f$, @f$n_{\rm hi}@f$ and thresholds @f$T_{\rm lo}@f$,
 *   @f$T_{\rm hi}@f$ (all configurable) retains only pixels above threshold.
 * - Extra pixels @f$N_{\rm before}@f$, @f$N_{\rm after}@f$ around each
 *   sequence are preserved.
 *
 * @}
 */

